{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And your offspring who shall be born after them</b> — If you beget more sons, they shall not be counted among \"my sons\" but shall be included within the tribes of Ephraim and Manasseh, and they shall have no name like the rest of the tribes with regard to inheritance. And even though the land of Israel was divided according to their head-count, as it is written, \"to the many you shall increase his inheritance\" (Numbers 26:54), and every man took an equal portion except for the firstborns, in any case only these are called tribes — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 48:6). And this is not correct, for if so, the birthright of Joseph is only a matter of honor, that his sons are called tribes, but the verse says \"for an inheritance\" (1 Chronicles 5:1), and in the Gemara they said: he equipped them for an inheritance like Reuben and Simeon and for nothing else, etc., as is found in Horayot (6b), and our Rabbis mentioned everywhere (Bava Batra 123a) that Joseph was the firstborn for inheritance and inherited a double portion like one firstborn according to the law of every firstborn, and his birthright was not merely to be called tribes, as the Rav said. From this we further learn that they did not divide the land among all the tribes of Israel according to their head-count, for if so, what is this birthright in the inheritance? And if we say that they gave each and every one of the sons of Joseph a double portion like every man of the rest of the tribes, this is not mentioned in the verse at all, and we do not find in Jacob that he gave a birthright to Joseph except that he said here, \"like Reuben and Simeon they shall be to me,\" and from this the verse says (1 Chronicles 5:1), \"but his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph the son of Israel\" — if so, they were fully like two tribes, and that is his birthright, and so are the words of the Sages everywhere (Bava Batra 123a). But the matter is not at all as the Rav said, that the land of Israel was divided among the tribes: they made twelve equal portions from it, and the tribe of Simeon, the smallest among them, took like the tribe of Judah, which was great in population, and Ephraim and Manasseh took like Reuben and Simeon, equally. And so they raised it in the Gemara in the chapter Yesh Nochalin (ibid.). And it is written (Ezekiel 47:13), \"You shall inherit the land according to the twelve tribes of Israel — Joseph, portions,\" and so Onkelos said (Onkelos on Genesis 49:22), \"Two tribes shall go forth from his sons, they shall receive a portion and an inheritance\" — that they are equal in receiving the inheritance. And the meaning of \"חולקא\" is a firstborn's portion, and \"אחסנתא\" is ordinary inheritance. And what the verse says (Numbers 26:54), \"to the many you shall increase his inheritance and to the few you shall diminish,\" concerns the ancestral houses mentioned in the section — the verse speaks of the tribe dividing its portion among the ancestral houses of those who came out of Egypt: to a populous ancestral house they give a large portion and to a small ancestral house they give a small portion, and the dead inherit through the living, as is explicit in Sifrei (Pinchas 132), and Rashi mentioned it in the portion of Pinchas (Numbers 26:55). And the general principle regarding Joseph is that he was the firstborn for inheritance: if Israel divided the land among the tribes as mentioned in the Gemara (Bava Batra 121a), they gave him like Reuben and Simeon, equally. And if we were to say that they divided it according to their head-count, as is evident from the verse, they gave them double according to their number — an ordinary portion according to the law of the rest of the men, and a second portion of firstborn-right, and the meaning of \"like Reuben and Simeon\" would be — double according to their number. But that Joseph should be like the rest of the tribes in the inheritance, and the birthright should merely be that they are called two tribes, as the Rav said — this is a thing impossible in any way:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {0 "The Rav (הרב) refers to Maimonides. The Horayot citation '(ו)' likely refers to 6b. The phrase 'והמתים יורשים החיים' means that inheritance was reckoned through the living descendants of those who died, i.e., the dead were represented by their living offspring in the division of the land."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:46:36.023509428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}